WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.460
He's been a fixture of every single big-screen
Batman franchise, but the cinematic attempts

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.460
at the Joker have never really lined up with
the version that we see in the comics.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:13.879
Even when they're great performances with
iconic visuals, there's always something that

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:14.980
the movies get wrong.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:20.340
We've all seen Thomas and Martha Wayne get
shot down in an alley about 47 times, so we

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:24.440
all know that superhero movies love to give
their viewers an origin story.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.660
It's not just for the good guys, either — almost
every villain who steps onto the screen brings

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:31.180
a whole backstory with them.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.679
Normally, that's not a problem, and can actually
give those villains the pathos that makes

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:36.960
for a compelling story.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.140
In the Joker's case, however, it's the exact
opposite.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:45.671
The biggest offender in this regard is, of
course, Tim Burton's 1989 Batman film, in

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.920
which Jack Nicholson took on the role of the
Clown Prince of Crime.

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:55.200
Not only do we get a full origin story, his
past gets tied back into Batman's own origin

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.000
so that we can wrap everything up in a nice
bow, which ultimately makes him less interesting.

00:01:00.409 --> 00:01:04.980
We see that he's a weird, catchphrase-spewing
homicidal maniac long before he tumbles into

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:05.940
a vat of acid.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.340
The only real difference, aside from an admittedly
iconic look, is that he gets slightly more

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:12.980
artistic with his murders.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.560
For a while, it seemed like the movies had
learned their lesson on this front.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:21.140
The Joker that we saw in The Dark Knight shows
up fully formed and even offers conflicting

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:24.040
versions of his own origin depending on who
he's talking to.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:26.880
"You want to know how I got these scars?"

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:34.300
Eve more recently, Jared Leto's version in
Suicide Squad is downright inexplicable in

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:35.500
pretty much every way.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:41.480
Now, though, we have an entire movie about
the Joker's origin, without any signs of Batman,

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:43.090
set for release this year.

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:47.050
Hopefully this time, they'll finally be able
to get rid of all that compelling mystery

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:49.120
he's been burdened with for so long!

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:54.340
Fans have long accepted the idea that it's
Batman who's Bruce Wayne's true identity,

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:57.840
and not the billionaire playboy alter-ego,
and it's a good rule of thumb.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:03.520
And yet, the movies treat the Joker, someone
who exists specifically in response to Batman,

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.380
as someone who always has to have that personal
connection.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:11.280
Admittedly, they do this with most of the
villains as a little bit of origin story shorthand.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:15.920
In the Joel Schumacher films, the Riddler
and Poison Ivy are both former employees of

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:19.120
Wayne Industries, out for revenge against
their old boss.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.160
The Joker, however, exists specifically to
be a counterpoint to Batman.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:28.560
The person under the mask is basically irrelevant
compared to the ideological battle being waged

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:29.560
against the symbol.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:34.340
That aspect of the Joker's character, however,
is continually missed by the films.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.900
In the '89 Batman, it's not enough that the
Joker's trying to murder the entire city with

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:44.220
a cloud of poison gas, there has to be a personal
connection to get the crimefighter into action.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:49.960
Even in 2019's Joker solo film, billed as
being more of a character study than a superhero

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:54.200
flick, Thomas Wayne appears as an antagonist
to provide that connection yet again.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:00.480
For all its flaws, the '89 Batman movie incorporates
one longtime, definitive element of the Joker

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.240
that no other film does.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.080
It's the one that remembers his trademark
is poison.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:09.100
"With a new secret ingredient, smiley."

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:10.760
… "What is that?"

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:15.980
In the comics, Joker Toxin dates back to the
character's very first appearance in 1940,

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.840
and has been a signature of the character
ever since.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:23.530
In addition to the outright creepiness of
a corpse with a smile frozen on its face,

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:27.140
there's nothing that captures the Joker's
mission statement quite as well as leaving

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:31.660
behind dead bodies in his own image, creating
a world with more Jokers.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:37.560
For obvious reasons, the campy, kid-friendly
1966 Batman film didn't involve a lot of poison

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:38.060
murders.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:42.680
When Heath Ledger offered up a performance
that defined the Joker for a new generation,

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:47.660
his weapons of choice were knives, explosives,
and complicated moral dilemmas, which left

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:48.820
very few people smiling.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:53.200
The Suicide Squad Joker seems to favor guns
and knives as well, which we know from the

00:03:53.209 --> 00:03:57.470
scene where he's laying on the floor in a
weird circle of them that also includes baby

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:00.040
clothes, champagne bottles, and a laptop.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:04.440
It's definitely a striking visual in its own
right, but it gets a little less sinister

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:08.680
once you start imagining Jared Leto spending
hours arranging it all to get it just right.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:13.500
One one level, you have to hand it to the
cinematic versions of the Joker: they always

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:14.880
set their sights pretty big.

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:20.860
In Batman '89, the Joker attempts to poison
the entirety of Gotham during the city's bicentennial,

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.220
using a deadly parade.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:23.920
"My balloons.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.400
Those are my balloons."

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:32.980
In The Dark Knight, he tries to trick a literal
boatload of citizens into blowing themselves

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:37.180
up, and that's after a series of high-profile
murders and the bombing of a hospital.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:41.840
The Joker of Suicide Squad doesn't get much
screen time, but it's already established

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:44.380
in Batman v Superman that he killed Robin.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:48.900
Even in the '60s version, the Joker's part
of a plot to vaporize every delegate to the

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.660
United Nations, at the height of the Cold
War!

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.140
Of course, this isn't really a departure from
the source material.

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:59.780
Since the '80s, the Joker's body count has
been ramped up to the extreme, resulting in

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:03.680
a character who's only slightly less lethal
than, say, the bubonic plague.

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:08.300
Even his attempt to take out the UN is something
that would eventually make its way into the

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:12.270
comics as part of the "A Death in the Family"
story, even if it's always overshadowed by

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:13.280
Robin being killed.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.980
The thing is, that's not the only thing the
Joker does.

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:23.320
While he has a long history of wild, theatrical
murders, the Joker is also known for his unpredictability.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:28.440
Sometimes he shows up and poisons everyone,
and sometimes he applies for a patent on smiling

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:31.180
fish and only turns to murder once he's denied.

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:34.840
Sometimes he tries to become the first criminal
to commit crimes on the moon.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:39.420
A lot of those stories might result from his
decades operating under the Comics Code, but

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:43.419
that bizarre unpredictability is what makes
the Joker work as more than just a slasher

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:44.420
movie bad guy.

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:49.760
The Joker wasn't the first over-the-top, thematic
crook to show up on the comics page, but if

00:05:49.761 --> 00:05:53.710
you're looking for a place to draw the line
between "villain" and "supervillain," he makes

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:54.800
a good place to start.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:05:59.160
"...Wait'll they get a load of me.”

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:09.740
His arrival in Batman #1 set the standard
for costumed criminals, and provided the blueprint

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:12.900
that supervillains are still using almost
80 years later.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:17.300
More often than not, though, movies treat
the Joker as just a slightly more colorful

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:19.960
representative of traditional organized crime.

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:24.600
While that was true for his first few appearances,
Joker became far more than that.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:29.460
In Batman '89, the Joker eventually winds
up in full-on supervillain mode — complete

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:34.040
with an army of henchmen in matching satin
jackets — but at heart, Jack Napier is a

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:38.340
gangster, and his first actions after his
transformation involve taking over the mob.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:42.330
The same goes for The Dark Knight, where the
Joker's introduction involves him taking a

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:47.250
contract for the murder of Batman, eventually
destabilizing the city's organized crime in

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:48.640
order to sow chaos.

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:50.360
"What do you propose."

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:53.500
"Well we kill the Batman.”

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:58.980
Suicide Squad might actually be the film that
goes furthest, showing the Joker as a mob

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:03.180
boss, holding court in a nightclub that caters
to facial tattoo enthusiasts.

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:08.620
Presumably, this is meant to tie the Joker
to a more realistic idea of crime, which seems

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:10.000
pretty unnecessary.

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:15.100
Are audiences only going to accept a genocidal
maniac who was turned into a clown by falling

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:19.729
into a pit of chemicals, who also fights a
man dressed up as a human-sized bat, only

00:07:19.729 --> 00:07:21.520
if we can relate him to The Sopranos?

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:26.280
This is a weird thing to say about the Joker,
but sometimes, less is more.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:31.440
Since his first live-action appearance in
1966, the design for the Joker in live action

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:33.440
has become more and more over the top.

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:38.419
From a visual standpoint, Jack Nicholson was
a more-or-less accurate re-creation of what

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:39.620
we'd seen in the comics.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:44.560
Heath Ledger combined the traditional purple
suit with weird, studiously sloppy face-paint

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:46.180
and his infamous scars.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:49.560
And Jared Leto… well, you know.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:54.330
The guy has "damaged" tattooed on his forehead,
and that is an aesthetic that doesn't even

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:57.280
exist in the same universe as the concept
of subtlety.

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:02.100
While that particular take on the Joker is
certainly memorable, it's not nearly as effective

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:07.259
as the relatively restrained Dark Knight version,
and not even close to being as iconic as the

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:09.340
simpler Batman '89 style.

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:14.080
When there's too much to focus on from a visual
standpoint, that visual loses its meaning

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:15.780
and just becomes a jumbled mess.

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:20.540
2019's Joker seems to be heading back in a
slightly more restrained direction.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:24.620
Joaquin Phoenix is back to the painted-on
clown makeup, but with the colorful purple

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:28.860
suit that matches his comic book counterpart's
established tradition pretty well.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:34.080
From a structural standpoint, one of the biggest
problems with the movie versions of the Joker

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:38.260
is more of a problem with movies themselves:
they only really use him once.

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:43.479
While he's certainly great as a one-shot villain,
the Joker is used best when he's a persistent

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:43.980
threat.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:48.480
The idea that he's always out there, able
to just stroll out of Arkham Asylum and begin

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.089
his next chaotic crime spree, is a really
cool part of his character, just like it is

00:08:53.089 --> 00:08:54.700
for any comic book supervillain.

00:08:55.270 --> 00:09:00.080
You never know who's going to make their shocking
return, or which mastermind might be manipulating

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:03.580
things from behind the scenes in order to
make the hero's life a nightmare.

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:08.160
That's when the Joker works best — when
his exit from the story is more ambiguous

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:12.740
than definitive, when no one finds the body,
and when you know he'll be back for another

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:13.240
round.

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:19.040
"I think you and I are destined to do this
forever."

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:23.750
Unfortunately, with the very recent exception
of the Marvel Cinematic Universe, movies are

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:25.440
meant to be more self-contained.

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:29.820
Having the Joker linger in the background
while a movie focused on less popular and

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:33.740
marketable villains would make about as much
sense as having Darkseid cooling his heels

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:38.860
offscreen while a C-list bad guy like Steppenwolf
fought the Justice League in their first big-screen

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:39.360
outing.

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:43.820
Interestingly enough, there was a thought
of doing this during the Dark Knight trilogy;

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:48.250
the Joker survives the events of the film,
and the novelization for The Dark Knight Rises

00:09:48.250 --> 00:09:51.900
even mentions that he is the last remaining
inmate of Arkham Asylum.

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:56.880
Unfortunately, Heath Ledger's tragic real-life
death prevented any of those plans from coming

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:00.640
to fruition, leaving his performance as a
singular take on the Joker.

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:02.220
The Joker's scary.

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:03.540
He has to be, right?

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:09.300
He's the ultimate creepy clown, a mass murderer
plotting death behind his smile, an unpredictable

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:12.840
foil for a hero determined to stop him at
almost any cost.

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:18.100
In all honesty, the movies have that one down
— the scene in Batman '89 where the Joker

00:10:18.110 --> 00:10:22.320
unwraps his face and then shatters a mirror,
laughing while a stammering doctor tries to

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:24.560
blame his work on a tray full of rusty tools?

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:28.200
That's still legitimately unsettling, and
a high point of the film.

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:32.120
That said, there's one other thing the Joker
ought to be: funny.

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:36.720
It really shouldn't be that difficult to pull
off, since horror and comedy both rely on

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:40.890
a punch of surprise to get their reaction,
but it's been a tricky balance for the movies

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:41.380
to walk.

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:46.360
The closest we've seen to nailing it is, once
again, Ledger's performance in The Dark Knight.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:51.500
"... hi."

00:10:52.460 --> 00:10:57.120
Ledger's Joker pulls off great, genuinely
funny bits of comedy in his performance without

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:59.240
ever losing that undercurrent of menace.

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:04.440
It's a necessary element, recasting Batman
as the straight man in an unintentional comedy

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:07.100
partnership that usually ends with someone
dead.

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:11.720
As for the others, well, Nicholson's version
wound up being more weird than funny, and

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:17.660
Leto's… well, that performance was hilarious,
but probably not in a way that was intentional.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:19.520
Still, has to count for something, right?

